y  at different stages so  either in the early endosome or late  endosome  or lysosome depending on the specific  biology of a given virus  now it's important to note that this  pathway is an acidic pathway  so viruses outside the cell are in a  neutral ph environment  as they enter an endosome they become  acidified and as they progress  to a late endosome or a lysosome that ph  can drop  down to around four and so we can take  advantage of that  to study the uptake and endocytosis and  maturation of  viral particles so in one example if we  take  enterovirus and label it with a ph  sensitive dye  called frodo ifl green the stp ester is  an amine reactive version of this dye  if you label a virus with this dye it  will be a non-fluorescent outside a cell  but it will become very brightly  fluorescent as its  endocytosed and as it progresses along  that endocytic  pathway and so the fluorescent signal  by the dye gives us a quantitative  measure of how  far into a cell that virus has  progressed  and in this case a drug treatment is  being used to inhibit  acidification of that pathway so we can  see some panels  where the signal is less bright now  we can perform a quantitative analysis  with the same type of ph sensitive dye  in this case using rio virus  labeled with the red version so frodo  ifl red  stp ester was used to label this virus  then it was added to hela cells for the  indicated times in this chart  and then with flow cytometry we can just  ask  how bright is each cell in that red  channel  and as we see in our  photo labeled and treated channel our  mean fluorescence intensity increases  quite substantially  you know three to four fold brighter  while if ammonium chloride is used to  alkalinize  those compartments our signal remains  minimal  and so using this this approach we can  effectively track the internalization of  cell viruses into cells so after  internalization  we'd like to study the encoding and  replication of viruses in host cells  now